When traveling abroad, get in the habit of drinking bottled water rather than tap water. It is a simple, inexpensive and readily available way to help you stay healthy.
Drink bottled water even in areas of good sanitation--northern Europe, Japan, Australia and New Zealand. The reason: water systems everywhere contain micro-organisms. While you are accustomed to those in your water at home, the ones in other countries may be sufficiently different to cause you mild intestinal upsets, especially the first few days you drink it.
Very likely, in areas of good sanitation, some of the intestinal upsets blamed on jet lag, overeating, rich and unusual food and too many alcoholic drinks are, in fact, water related. Who needs that?
And in areas of questionable sanitation--the rest of the world--where the incidence of intestinal upsets among visitors ranges from 10 to 50 percent, estimates are that water is the cause of about two-thirds of the problems. In the Soviet Union, for example, many water systems contain a diarrhea-causing organism, giardia, which generally causes symptoms a week or two after you ingest it--often after you are back home from your trip.
Also, in most developing countries, little is known about chemical contaminants in the water. Very likely, such contaminants are present in some areas.
Also important for your health overseas is the water used in kitchens of hotels and restaurants for washing dishes, cleansing vegetables, making ice cubes and for other purposes.
In areas of good sanitation, tap water is generally used in kitchens. In such areas, the type and number of micro-organisms contained in the limited amount of tap water from these sources are unlikely to make you ill.
This, however, is not true in areas of questionable sanitation. There, even the limited amount of water that you swallow from ice cubes, cleansing vegetables and washing dishes may make you ill.
In such areas your best bet for avoiding intestinal illnesses is staying and eating at the de luxe hotels of large, international hotel chains. Generally, these hotels have elaborate protocols to insure safe water: local water is refiltered, additional chlorine is added, the hotel itself runs bacterial analyses of its water from various places within the hotel and samples are sent for further analysis to independent outside laboratories.
In these hotels only such water is used for making ice cubes, washing dishes and cleansing vegetables.
In restaurants in areas of questionable sanitation--even ones with good reputations and that cater to tourists--avoid ice cubes and salads and drink bottled water only. The reason: you have no way of knowing what kind of water is being used. However, dishes and silverware at these restaurants are generally safe.
In areas of good sanitation and in de luxe hotels in other areas, tap water is generally safe for brushing teeth. Elsewhere, use bottled water.
In India, China and in some other developing countries many hotels provide open canisters of water in your room. As you have no way of knowing where the water comes from, use bottled water.
Bottled water is available in all hotels and restaurants that cater to tourists. Bottles are less expensive at local stores than in the hotels.
Besides bottled water, other safe beverages to drink are beer, wine, liquors, carbonated drinks, coffee and tea.
In areas of questionable sanitation, especially in the tropics, when going on excursions, carry bottled water and soft drinks. Also carry paper cups and straws. If you buy soft drinks or water from local establishments, drink the beverage from its container rather than glasses or cups provided by the establishment.
In areas of questionable sanitation be suspect of juices and fruit drinks not prepared in your presence. Tap water may be added.
Use only water you would drink for other hygienic purposes: cleaning contact lenses, rinsing dentures and irrigating colostomies, for example.
Very handy for brushing teeth and making tea or coffee in your room is an electric immersion coil. Boiling water for five minutes will destroy virtually all micro-organisms. Depending on the country, you need a current converter and a plug adapter for your coil.
One caution about bottled water: If you are on a special diet, check the label for ingredients. Some waters contain high concentrations of sodium, calcium and other minerals.

Dandelions Stars in the Grass
To anyone intent on a perfectly manicured lawn, dandelions are known as a weed and a nuisance. In Dandelions, Posada offers a different view of this ubiquitous yellow flower.
With a simple rhyming text she presents the flower as a fascinating and cheerful harbinger of spring. The soft, predominantly green and yellow illustrations leave no white space on the page and are full of close-up views of insects and dandelions. The journey of the seeds is shown, as they float like umbrellas across the meadow to land on a hillside and begin the growing process all over again.
The book includes additional information about dandelions, such as the origin of the name, which is from the French “dent de lion,” or lion’s tooth. There is also a recipe for salad and a page or two of science experiments. While not as in-depth as Lauber’s Seeds: Pop, Stick, Glide, Dandelions is a good introduction for young children who are just discovering the mystery of seeds.
Although this is a common flower that grows almost everywhere, readers of Dandelion will gain a new appreciation for this “star in the grass.”
Disclosure: This article is not an endorsement, but a review. The author of this book provided free copies of the book to have their book reviewed by a professional reviewer. No fee was paid by the author for this review. Foreword Reviews only recommends books that we love. Foreword Magazine, Inc. is disclosing this in accordance with the Federal Trade Commission’s 16 CFR, Part 255.

